The code statements that lead to the bug are:
raid5_release_stripe(sh);
Reason:
In the failure path of `retry_aligned_read()`, calling `raid5_release_stripe(sh)` puts the stripe on the lockless `released_stripes` llist. In the next `raid5d` loop, `release_stripe_list()` moves the stripe to `handle_list` because `STRIPE_HANDLE` is set. Then `retry_aligned_read()` runs again (before `handle_active_stripes()`) and calls `find_get_stripe()`, which removes the stripe from `handle_list` via `list_del_init()`, preventing `handle_stripe()` from ever processing it. This creates an infinite loop and causes a soft lockup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
spin_lock_irq(&conf->device_lock);
hash = sh->hash_lock_index;
__release_stripe(conf, sh, &conf->temp_inactive_list[hash]);
spin_unlock_irq(&conf->device_lock);
Reason:
By using `__release_stripe()` with the `temp_inactive_list` instead of `raid5_release_stripe()`, the stripe is not placed on the `released_stripes` llist. This prevents it from being moved to `handle_list` and subsequently removed by `find_get_stripe()` before `handle_stripe()` can process it. The overlap will be resolved when the stripe is eventually handled by `handle_stripe()`, breaking the infinite loop and avoiding the soft lockup.

